  "full_response": "**Eventual Consistency** is a consistency model used in distributed systems to achieve high availability and partition tolerance (as per the **CAP Theorem**). It guarantees that if no new updates are made to a given data item, eventually all accesses to that item will return the last updated value. However, it does not provide immediate consistency across all nodes in the system.\n\n### **Key Characteristics of Eventual Consistency:**\n1. **No Immediate Guarantees**\n   - Unlike **strong consistency** (where all reads return the most recent write), eventual consistency allows temporary inconsistencies.\n   - Different replicas (copies of data) may return different values for a short period.\n\n2. **Convergence Over Time**\n   - If updates stop, all replicas will eventually synchronize and reflect the same value.\n   - This is achieved through background synchronization mechanisms (e.g., gossip protocols, anti-entropy processes).\n\n3. **Trade-off for Performance & Availability**\n   - Systems prioritize **availability** and **partition tolerance** over immediate consistency.\n   - Useful in large-scale distributed systems (e.g., DNS, CDNs, NoSQL databases like Cassandra, DynamoDB).\n\n### **When is Eventual Consistency Used?**\n- **Highly Available Systems** (e.g., social media feeds, e-commerce product catalogs).\n- **Geographically Distributed Databases** (e.g., multi-region cloud storage).\n- **Systems with High Read/Write Throughput** (e.g., caching layers, messaging systems).\n\n### **Examples of Eventual Consistency in Action:**\n- **DNS (Domain Name System):** Changes to DNS records take time to propagate globally.\n- **Amazon S3:** Updates to objects may not be immediately visible across all regions.\n- **Cassandra & DynamoDB:** Allow reads to return stale data temporarily for better performance.\n\n### **Challenges with Eventual Consistency:**\n- **Stale Reads:** Clients may see outdated data.\n- **Conflict Resolution:** Requires mechanisms (e.g., vector clocks, last-write-wins) to resolve conflicting updates.\n- **Application Complexity:** Developers must handle temporary inconsistencies in their logic.\n\n### **Eventual Consistency vs. Strong Consistency**\n| Feature               | Eventual Consistency | Strong Consistency |\n|-----------------------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| **Read Latency**      | Low (fast reads)     | High (slower reads)|\n| **Write Latency**     | Low (fast writes)    |",
